window.docContents[230].resolve({"/Tier-1___-statements/Theorem-files/Computational.Theorems.Lemmas/#file-Computational___Theorems___Lemmas":{"contents":"Paper › Computational/Theorems › Lemmas.leanTier 1 · theorem file\n\nTier 1 · theorem file used by the computational proof 4 headline theorems 93 lines trust banner B1, trusted: the statement source ↗Read in full. This file states headline theorems. Proofs are cut after := and shown as ⋯; Lean checks them.\n\nCited in the paper* Lemma 1 (PQXDH), §C.3: headlineδ* Lemma 2 (Single session), §C.3: session_secure* Lemma 3 (Composition), §C.3: model_secure_costed\n\n\n\nLemmas 1–3 and Kyber-1024 δ_kem: PQXDH, Single Session, Composition, and KEM CorrectnessTrusted: the statement. Each theorem below states a proposition from Computational/Specifications/ and is proved by a corresponding *_proof theorem in Computational/Proofs/. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md for the trusted definitions and kernel axioms of each theorem, and Computational/Theorems/Main.lean (CliElfLib) for Lemma 4 and the Main Theorem on the compiled signal-session binary.* Lemma* Theorem* Statement* Proof* 1 (PQXDH)* SignalPqxdh.Security.headlineδ* PqxdhCorrectAndSecureδ (Computational/Specifications/Pqxdh/Security/HeadlineDelta.lean)* Computational/Proofs/SignalPqxdh/Delta/Statements.lean* 2 (single session)* CryptoVerification.E2E.session_secure* SessionSecure (Computational/Specifications/Session/StatementSession.lean)* Computational/Proofs/LibsignalCli/HeadlineSession.lean* 3 (composition)* CryptoVerification.E2E.model_secure_costed* ModelSecure (Computational/Specifications/Composition/StatementModel.lean)* Computational/Proofs/LibsignalCli/HeadlineModel.lean* Kyber-1024 δ_kem* CryptoVerification.E2E.kyber1024_kem_delta_correct* Kyber1024KemDeltaCorrect (Computational/Specifications/LibsignalCli/Kyber1024Delta.lean)* Computational/Proofs/Kyber1024Delta/HeadlineProof.lean\n\n\n\ntheorem headlineδL44–55 ↗#Lemma 1 (PQXDH) (PqxdhCorrectAndSecureδ, Computational/Specifications/Pqxdh/Security/HeadlineDelta.lean). For all setups S and well-formed primitives P (WellFormedPrimitivesδ P): honest Alice and Bob derive matching session keys (PqxdhCorrect P); the one-key handshake with HkdfRO satisfies PqxdhKeyIndSecureδ S, bounding KeyInd1KeyRoAdvantage by pqxdhKeyIndAdvantageBoundActiveδ (pqxdhKeyIndBoundActive plus kemDecapsFailureTerm) via costed reductions to GapSigEufCma, KemShCr, and GapDhWithSign; and the one-key first-message authentication game satisfies PqxdhAuth1KeySecureδ S (Computational/Specifications/Pqxdh/Security/Auth/Headline.lean, DEV-C5), bounding Auth1KeyAdvantage by pqxdhAuthAdvantageBoundδ via costed reductions to GapSigEufCma, KemShCr, GapDhWithSign, PrfChainKey, PrfMsgKeys, AeadIndCpa, and PrfHmac.\n\n\n\ntheorem session_secureL63–71 ↗#Lemma 2 (single session) (SessionSecure, Computational/Specifications/Session/StatementSession.lean). There exists a family D of cost-bounded session reductions such that, for all primitives satisfying the session hypotheses (P1–P10), a 32-bit counter bound, and a point-fiber bound δ, every mixture oa of session contexts and q-query adversaries has single-session SM.gameR bias against sessionSchemeFixedSpk at most sessionAdvantageBound evaluated on D.\n\ntheorem model_secure_costedL73–82 ↗#Lemma 3 (composition) (ModelSecure, Computational/Specifications/Composition/StatementModel.lean). For the one-key real-HKDF handshake ki := SignalPqxdh.pqxdhKeyIndImpl1KeyReal S P, there exists a budget-indexed family of cost-bounded ReductionDsl reductions (uniform in P) such that, for every P satisfying SignalPqxdh.WellFormedPrimitivesδ P, any bounded adversary's advantage in G(Σ_model^ki) is at most twice the KeyInd advantage against ki plus, per promoted session, the supremum over flip of the single-session SM.gameR bias mixed over stage 1 of the prefix reduction.\n\ntheorem kyber1024_kem_delta_correctL84–91 ↗#Kyber-1024 δ_kem correctness (Kyber1024KemDeltaCorrect, Computational/Specifications/LibsignalCli/Kyber1024Delta.lean). At realPEParams and kyber1024KemKeyGen, the PQXDH model primitives modelPrims (realUnreachedPrims kyber1024KemKeyGen) realPEParams produce (1568B, 3168B) key pairs on every seed and, for any ε satisfying the CBD-noise idealization hypothesis SignalPqxdh.KyberNoiseGap ε, satisfy SignalPqxdh.KemDeltaCorrect P (ε + 1 / 2 ^ 112).\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"Computational/Theorems/Lemmas.lean","id":"/Tier-1___-statements/Theorem-files/Computational.Theorems.Lemmas/#file-Computational___Theorems___Lemmas"},"/Tier-2___-definitions/Computational___Specifications___/Computational.Specifications.Assumptions.KeyInd1KeyRo/#file-Computational___Specifications___Assumptions___KeyInd1KeyRo":{"contents":"Paper › Computational/Specifications/Assumptions › KeyInd1KeyRo.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof 2 headline theorems depend on it 20 of 63 lines trusted trust banner B2, trusted: definitions used by the statement source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nKey indistinguishability with one-key identities and a random-oracle key derivationTrusted: definitions used by the statement. Lemma 1 (PqxdhCorrectAndSecureδ, Security/HeadlineDelta.lean) bounds KeyInd1KeyRoAdvantage on pqxdhKeyIndImpl1KeyRo, and clause 2 of the PQXDH CLI statements (e.g. CliPqxdhElfSecure) uses KeyIndRoGap. Read with Computational/Specifications/Pqxdh/Game.lean (pqxdhKeyIndImpl), Assumptions.lean (GapDhWithSign, KemShCr, GapSigEufCma), and KeyInd1KeyReal.lean. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md and Computational/docs/DEVIATIONS.md (DEV-CLI16).The CLI statements relate the real one-key handshake game (KeyInd1KeyRealAdvantage) to the one-key random-oracle game (KeyInd1KeyRoAdvantage, replacing P.hkdfDerive96 with HkdfRO, DEV-PQ14) via KeyIndRoGap, while Lemma 1 bounds KeyInd1KeyRoAdvantage under GapSigEufCma, KemShCr, and GapDhWithSign. Against pqxdhKeyIndImpl1KeyReal, pqxdhKeyIndImpl1KeyRo differs only in using evalHkdfOrRo true (HkdfRO) rather than evalHkdfOrRo false (P.hkdfDerive96), as checked in Computational/Tests/Guards/KeyInd1KeyRoGuard.lean.\n\n\n\ndef pqxdhKeyIndImpl1KeyRoL40–48 ↗#Key-indistinguishability oracle with single-scalar identities and random-oracle key derivation. Delegates .initPrin u to pqxdhKeyIndImpl1KeyReal and all other queries to pqxdhKeyIndImpl true S P, which uses one shared lazy HkdfRO cache (evalHkdfOrRo true) across .roQuery, initiate*, and accept* and disables .revealDhSecrets.\n\ndef KeyInd1KeyRoAdvantageL50–54 ↗#Distinguishing advantage of A in pqxdhKeyInd1KeyExpForHandshake against pqxdhKeyIndImpl1KeyRo.\n\ndef KeyIndRoGapL56–61 ↗#Random-oracle gap (HkdfRO, DEV-PQ14) on the one-key handshake game for A: bounds KeyInd1KeyRealAdvantage by KeyInd1KeyRoAdvantage + ε. Takes ε = 0 in the random-oracle model of FG24 and BJKS24 (pqxdhKeyIndAdvantageBoundActiveδ); remains an unproved hypothesis in the standard model (DEV-CLI16).\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"Computational/Specifications/Assumptions/KeyInd1KeyRo.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/Computational___Specifications___/Computational.Specifications.Assumptions.KeyInd1KeyRo/#file-Computational___Specifications___Assumptions___KeyInd1KeyRo"}});